<ArticleLayout title="The Verified Homeowner Quote Project" description="A transparent foundation for future installed-cost evidence, with no fabricated submissions or averages." category="Cost" categoryHref="/cost/" published="2026-10-05" updated="2026-10-07" verified="2026-10-05" readingTime="8 min read" toc={[]} sources={sources}>

<h2>Real homeowner quote database coming as verified submissions are collected.</h2><p>There are currently <strong>zero verified homeowner submissions</strong> in this database. We do not publish synthetic quotes as real projects, infer local averages from equipment prices, or turn an unverified total into an installed-cost benchmark.</p>
<h2>What a useful submission needs</h2><p>Future records will capture metro or partial ZIP, state, quote date, exact generator and fuel, ATS, service size, gas-run length, trenching, pad, load management, permits, utility work, electrical upgrades, accessories and project total. Verification status and missing scope must remain visible alongside any future statistics.</p>
<h2>Collection is not open yet</h2><p>No quote-upload form is active. Before collection opens, the service needs authenticated administration, private document storage, malware and file-type checks, rate limits, server-side schema validation, consent records, redaction, a retention policy and a correction/deletion process. Names, addresses, account numbers and original documents must not appear in public records.</p>
<p>The <a href="/planning/quotes/">three-quote tool</a> is available now. It saves only when requested, in your own browser; it does not submit a quote to this project.</p>
<h2>Privacy-safe pilot preparation you can use now</h2>
<p>We now publish a <a href="/templates/standby-quote-pilot-blank.csv" download>blank quote-comparison research template (CSV)</a> for homeowners who wish to organize their own proposals. It includes <strong>no submissions, survey responses, amounts, sample statistics or original documents</strong>. Download and keep it on your device; there is no upload or collection endpoint.</p>
<p>Only enter general, non-identifying scope notes if you create a local copy. Do not record names, address, full ZIP, account numbers, signatures or original document text. <strong>Do not email the completed CSV or private proposal documents to this project.</strong> Collection will open only after consent, secure storage, retention and deletion processes are independently checked.</p>
<h2>How future comparisons should work</h2><p>Only verified, consented, redacted records should enter a published analysis. Report sample size, date range, geography, configuration mix and missing scope. Separate quotes from completed invoices and changes after installation. Small or biased samples cannot establish a universal fair price.</p>

<h2>What verification will mean</h2><ol><li>Review an original, dated proposal or completed invoice, exact equipment and fuel, totals and scope. A quote is an offer; an invoice documents a billed project, not necessarily payment or satisfactory performance.</li><li>Confirm permission for anonymous publication and flag missing lines. Unknown costs stay unknown; bundled prices are not split into invented estimates.</li><li>Redact names, full addresses, contact details, signatures, account numbers, serial numbers, payment information, document metadata and identifying free text.</li><li>Check a private document fingerprint and project linkage. Multiple bids and revisions for one home must not become independent households. Quote/invoice pairs remain linked internally and separate in analysis.</li><li>Record the reviewer decision, verification date, redaction status, retention deadline and corrections history. Verification checks the document; it does not certify the contractor or price fairness.</li></ol>
<h2>What we will never publish</h2><p>Original documents, contact details, full ZIP codes, precise locations and private document fingerprints will not be included in public data. Even a three-digit ZIP or metro will be suppressed where a small group could identify a household. The public dataset must be a separately reviewed allowlist export, never a copy of intake records.</p>
<h2>Publication rules before the first chart</h2><p>Our initial editorial suppression floor is 20 independent verified projects per published cohort. This is a privacy and stability rule, not statistical proof. Below it, results stay suppressed. Above it, editors must still review selection bias, geographic concentration, missing scope and equipment mix.</p><p>Future tables must show sample size, median, first and third quartiles, range, period, fuel, state or metro, generator sizes and exact-configuration mix. Quotes and invoices will never be pooled. No national average is established. A convenience sample cannot represent all US homeowners, regardless of its size.</p>
<h2>How this connects to original research</h2><p>Read the <a href="/research/">study publication framework</a>. Collection remains closed until secure storage, document handling, consent, retention and deletion controls have been implemented and reviewed. Browser-local planning is separate and will never silently enroll a homeowner in research.</p>
